## 4. USER ROLES AND PERMISSIONS

Build a secure role-based access control system.

Required roles:

1. Super Administrator
2. School Administrator
3. Head Teacher / Principal
4. Deputy Head Teacher
5. Director / School Owner
6. Bursar / Accounts Officer
7. Cashier
8. Class Teacher
9. Subject Teacher
10. Academic Registrar
11. Admissions Officer
12. Human Resource Officer
13. Librarian
14. Transport Officer
15. Storekeeper / Inventory Manager
16. Boarding / Hostel Warden
17. School Nurse or authorized health staff
18. Counselor / Welfare Officer
19. Parent / Guardian
20. Student
21. Other configurable non-teaching staff

### Permission requirements

Implement granular permissions, not just role names.

Examples:

- Administrators can manage school settings and authorized users.
- Head teachers can review school-wide academic and attendance information.
- Deputy head teachers can manage assigned academic and operational responsibilities.
- Bursars can manage fee structures, invoices, payments and financial reports.
- Cashiers can record authorized payments but cannot alter fee structures or approve their own refunds.
- Class teachers can manage attendance and records for their assigned classes.
- Subject teachers can manage assessments for assigned subjects and classes.
- Admissions officers can process applications and enrollment.
- Librarians can manage books and borrowing.
- Transport officers can manage vehicles, routes and student allocations.
- Parents can access only the records of children legally linked to their verified accounts.
- Students can access only their own authorized information.
- Health, counseling and safeguarding records must have specially restricted permissions.

### Permission controls

Support permissions such as:

- View
- Create
- Edit
- Approve
- Publish
- Delete or archive
- Export
- Print
- Manage settings

Allow administrators to assign users to roles and, where necessary, grant limited additional permissions.

Apply permissions to individual API operations and sensitive records.

Never depend on hiding frontend buttons for security.

Record important permission changes in the audit log.

